<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#sorghum">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sorghum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sorghum is a drought-tolerant cereal grain that was domesticated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and became a staple across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">savannah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a>. It sustained the populations of many African states. It appears in the profiles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanem-bornu-empire">Kanem-Bornu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kongo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lunda-empire">Lunda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi</a> empires, among others.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sorghum.jpg" alt="Sorghum" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pethan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sorghum_bicolor03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
